# V-2 — L-12's timeout evidence is false; the real defect is worse

**From:** PARĪKṢAKA · **Verdict:** `VERDICTS.jsonl` id `V-2` · **Verdict:** FAIL (correction, not retraction)

## What the plan says

§1.9 **L-12** (evidence for **D-28**, "Nothing guarantees a run finishes"):
> default writer timeout 600 s against measured 30 h heavy builds unless `writer_timeout_seconds`
> is set per asset (`runner.py:89`; **set on ~2 of 128**)

## What live production says

- `writer_timeout_seconds` is **non-null on 128 of 128**.
- The column has `DEFAULT 600`, so the meaningful reading is *non-default* — and that is
  **107 of 128**, not 2. Wrong by ~50×.
- Distribution: `10800s ×91 · 600s ×21 · 60s ×5 · 120s ×5 · 1800s ×2 · 300s ×1 · 3600s ×1 ·
  21600s ×1 (ka_gochara_sweep) · 86400s ×1 (ka_kshetra)`.

## The defect survives, in a sharper and more dangerous form

Joining live timeouts against each asset's own measured runtime (D-13 POLLUTED rows excluded),
**7 assets have a timeout below their own measured worst run:**

| asset | timeout | measured worst | ratio | tier |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `ka_gochara` | 0.50 h | 6.50 h | **13.0×** | X · generation-bearing |
| `bo_laksana` | 3.00 h | 19.20 h | **6.4×** | H · heavy |
| `ka_gochara_sweep` | 6.00 h | 35.60 h | **5.9×** | X · generation-bearing |
| `ga_strength` | 3.00 h | 13.10 h | **4.4×** | H · heavy |
| `ga_sensitive` | 3.00 h | 6.40 h | 2.1× | H · heavy |
| `bo_laksana_rerank` | 0.17 h | 0.35 h | 2.1× | H · heavy |
| `ka_kshetra` | 24.00 h | 33.80 h | 1.4× | H · heavy |

`bo_laksana_rerank` is under its own **p90** (600 s vs 20 m) — the watchdog is expected to kill
it more than one run in ten, not merely in the tail.

**So the honest statement is not "timeouts are unset."** It is: *timeouts are set, deliberately,
on 107 of 128 — and seven of them, concentrated in exactly the heavy and generation-bearing
tiers, are set below the asset's own measured worst case.* A set-but-too-low ceiling is worse
than an unset one, because it reads as honest configuration while reliably killing the longest
builds. It is a plausible direct contributor to the 45.6 % completion rate L-12 attributes to
the opposite cause.

## A second, separate failure this exposed

The generated §15 **`timeout source`** field reads `registered` for **all 128 assets** —
including the 21 sitting on the bare column default. §15 defines the vocabulary as
`registered | telemetry-derived | default`. Because the generator tests only non-null and the
column defaults to 600, that field **can never emit `default` or `telemetry-derived`**. It is a
constant wearing the clothes of a measurement — an §N.8 / H4 earned-signal violation inside the
campaign's own control surface, the same class as V-1's missing hotspot. Both live in
`asset_plans.py` and should be fixed in one pass.

## Why it matters now

§8.6 stage 2 ("derive timeouts") is a per-rung conform task. With this, three rungs open with a
**named, quantified** timeout defect instead of a generic instruction:

- **R1** — `ga_strength`, `ga_sensitive`
- **R2** — `bo_laksana`, `bo_laksana_rerank`
- **R3** — `ka_gochara`, `ka_gochara_sweep`, `ka_kshetra`

## Ask

- **ADHIKĀRIN:** L-12/D-28's cited evidence is false while the defect is real. Restating a
  defect's evidence is not a G-power I hold and not obviously any of G1–G11 — your call whether
  the plan text and the Defect Register D-28 row are corrected now or at the rung that owns each
  asset. I recommend correcting D-28's evidence cell now, because a defect register that
  mis-describes its own defect will mislead every KĀRAKA that reads it.
- **SŪTRADHĀRA:** the seven assets above are concrete stage-2 conform work, already measured.

Read-only throughout. No file changed, no build dispatched. Verdict only (I16).
